DESC = {
"fortran": "IBM built Fortran (Formula Translation) in the mid-1950s under John Backus because programming the IBM 704 in assembly was slow and costly. The goal was to let scientists write formulas and still get code as fast as hand-written assembly, and the compiler pulled that off. Its strengths are number crunching over large arrays and decades of tuned libraries. Its weaknesses are dated syntax, poor text handling and few new programmers. It still runs weather and climate models, physics simulations and the linear-algebra libraries underneath NumPy, R and MATLAB.",
"lisp": "John McCarthy designed Lisp at MIT in 1958 as a mathematical notation for computation, aimed at artificial-intelligence research. Programs and data share one form, nested lists in parentheses, so Lisp code can easily write and transform other code. It introduced garbage collection, the REPL and functions as values. Its strengths are flexibility and powerful macros; its weaknesses are the wall of parentheses, a fragmented family of dialects and a small job market. It lives on in Common Lisp niches, Emacs, Clojure and Scheme, and in ideas nearly every modern language borrowed.",
"cobol": "COBOL (Common Business-Oriented Language) was designed in 1959 by a committee convened by the US Department of Defense, building on Grace Hopper's FLOW-MATIC, to give businesses one portable language for records, money and reports. It reads like English (ADD TAX TO TOTAL), does exact decimal arithmetic and processes huge batches reliably. It is verbose, awkward outside data processing, and its programmers are retiring. Billions of lines still run bank ledgers, insurance, payroll and government benefit systems, mostly on IBM mainframes.",
"algol": "ALGOL was designed by an international committee of European and American computer scientists (1958 to 1960, revised as ALGOL 68) as a clean, machine-independent language for describing algorithms. It introduced block structure, nested scopes, recursion as standard and the BNF notation still used to define languages. It never became commercially dominant: IBM pushed Fortran, ALGOL 60 had no standard input and output, and ALGOL 68 was seen as too complex. It was used mainly in academia and for publishing algorithms, yet Pascal, C, Simula and Java all descend from it.",
"apl": "Kenneth Iverson created APL as a mathematical notation at Harvard and turned it into a language at IBM in the 1960s. It works on whole arrays at once with single-character symbols, so a statistical calculation can fit on one line. That makes it extraordinarily concise for exploring data, but it needs a special character set, reads right to left and is famously hard for outsiders to read. It survives in finance and insurance niches (Dyalog APL), its descendants k and q power kdb+ trading databases, and its array thinking shaped S, R, MATLAB and NumPy.",
"pascal": "Niklaus Wirth designed Pascal at ETH Zurich around 1970 to teach structured programming clearly and to be easy to compile. It is strongly typed, readable and strict, with records and clean control structures. Standard Pascal was too restrictive for real systems work (no separate compilation, weak strings), gaps that commercial dialects such as Turbo Pascal and Delphi filled. It was the dominant teaching language of the 1970s and 1980s and was used for Apple Lisa and early Mac software and for TeX.",
"forth": "Chuck Moore developed Forth around 1970 to control radio telescopes at the US National Radio Astronomy Observatory on machines with very little memory. Everything works through a stack in postfix order (2 3 +), and you extend the language by defining new words, so a whole system can be tiny and interactive. That suits small embedded hardware, but the code is hard to read for anyone but its author and the ecosystem is small. It has been used in boot firmware (Open Firmware), spacecraft and embedded controllers, and its idea lives on in PostScript.",
"c": "Dennis Ritchie created C at Bell Labs in 1972 to rewrite the Unix operating system in something higher-level than assembly but nearly as fast. It gives direct control over memory through pointers, maps closely to hardware, and has a tiny core with compilers for virtually every chip. That power is also its weakness: manual memory management makes crashes and security holes easy. C runs operating systems, databases, embedded devices and the interpreters of Python and R, and its syntax is the template for C++, Java, JavaScript and Go.",
"smalltalk": "Alan Kay's group at Xerox PARC created Smalltalk in the 1970s as a language children and non-specialists could use on personal computers; the same team pioneered the graphical user interface. Everything is an object that receives messages, and you program inside a live environment where anything can be changed while it runs. It is elegant and very productive but lives in its own world (an image file rather than plain text files), which made integration hard and slowed adoption. Pharo and Squeak serve enthusiasts and some finance systems, and its ideas shaped Objective-C, Java, Ruby and Python.",
"prolog": "Alain Colmerauer and colleagues in Marseille created Prolog in 1972 for natural-language processing, building on Robert Kowalski's logic programming. You state facts and rules and Prolog searches for answers that satisfy a query, instead of you writing step-by-step instructions. It excels at rules, parsing and symbolic reasoning but is slow and awkward for numeric or input-heavy programs, and its search can run away unpredictably. It is used in research, teaching, expert systems and language tools, including parts of IBM Watson.",
"ml": "Robin Milner created ML (Meta Language) at the University of Edinburgh in 1973 as the scripting language of a theorem prover, a system for proving programs correct. It introduced type inference, where the compiler works out every type itself, together with algebraic data types and pattern matching, giving static safety without heavy annotations. As a language it stayed academic, with a small ecosystem and little industry use. Standard ML is used in teaching and proof assistants, and its descendants OCaml and F# and its ideas in Rust, Swift, Scala and Haskell carry it into industry.",
"sql": "Donald Chamberlin and Raymond Boyce designed SQL at IBM in 1974 so people could query Edgar Codd's relational databases without writing loops. You describe the result you want (SELECT, FROM, WHERE) and the database decides how to compute it, which makes queries short, optimisable and readable by analysts. Every database speaks a slightly different dialect, complex logic gets clumsy, and procedural work needs extensions such as PL/SQL or T-SQL. It is the universal language of data, from banking systems to BigQuery, Databricks and Power BI.",
"scheme": "Gerald Sussman and Guy Steele created Scheme at MIT in 1975 as a minimal, clean dialect of Lisp for exploring models of computation. It has a tiny core with lexical scope, closures and proper tail calls, so a few powerful ideas go a long way, and the textbook SICP made it famous in teaching. Its minimalism led to many incompatible implementations and a small library ecosystem. It is used in teaching, research and as an embedded scripting language (Guile, Racket), and its design shaped JavaScript, R and Lua.",
"s": "John Chambers and colleagues created S at Bell Labs in 1976 so statisticians could analyse data interactively instead of writing Fortran programs. It treated data, models and graphics as objects you could explore at a prompt, calling fast Fortran routines underneath. Its commercial version, S-PLUS, was expensive and closed, which left room for a free alternative. It was used in statistics departments and industry through the 1990s and won the ACM Software System Award in 1998; R, its open-source successor, has replaced it.",
"matlab": "Cleve Moler wrote MATLAB (Matrix Laboratory) in the 1970s at the University of New Mexico so his students could use Fortran linear-algebra libraries without writing Fortran; MathWorks commercialised it in 1984. Everything is a matrix, the syntax is close to mathematical notation, and its toolboxes and Simulink are excellent for engineering and control systems. It is expensive, closed and weaker than Python as a general-purpose language. It is used in engineering, signal processing, control systems and universities, though Python has taken much of its academic ground.",
"objc": "Brad Cox and Tom Love created Objective-C in the early 1980s by adding Smalltalk-style messages to C. NeXT, Steve Jobs's company, adopted it, and Apple inherited it for macOS and later iOS. It combines C's speed with dynamic, flexible objects, but its square-bracket syntax is unusual, it is verbose, and it keeps C's memory pitfalls. It was the language of Mac and iPhone apps until Swift took over from 2014, and much of Apple's older code is still Objective-C.",
"cpp": "Bjarne Stroustrup created C++ at Bell Labs from 1979 (released 1985) to combine Simula's classes with C's speed. Templates, classes and automatic resource management let you write high-level code that compiles to fast machine code, which C++ calls zero-cost abstractions. It is huge and complex, easy to misuse, and inherits C's memory-safety problems, so slow builds and security bugs are recurring pain points. It powers game engines, browsers, trading systems, databases, Adobe and Office software and the cores of machine-learning frameworks.",
"excel": "Microsoft released Excel in 1985 (first for the Mac), and its formula language grew out of earlier spreadsheets such as VisiCalc and Lotus 1-2-3. Formulas are live: change a cell and everything recalculates, which makes it the most widely used programming environment in the world. Its weaknesses are hidden errors, logic scattered across cells and poor version control; LAMBDA (2020) finally lets users define their own reusable functions. It runs budgets, forecasts, financial models and ad-hoc analysis in almost every company.",
"erlang": "Joe Armstrong, Robert Virding and Mike Williams developed Erlang at Ericsson in 1986 to program telephone switches that must never go down. It runs millions of lightweight processes that share nothing and pass messages, follows a 'let it crash' philosophy where supervisors restart failed parts, and can upgrade code without downtime. It is excellent for concurrency and fault tolerance but weak at raw number crunching, with an unusual Prolog-like syntax and a small talent pool. It is used in telecoms and messaging (WhatsApp, RabbitMQ), and through Elixir in web back ends.",
"perl": "Larry Wall created Perl in 1987 while working at Unisys, to make report processing and text manipulation easier than combining shell, sed and awk. It made regular expressions a first-class feature and lets you do a lot in very little code, under the motto 'There's more than one way to do it'. That flexibility is also its weakness: Perl code is often hard to read, and the long Perl 6 (Raku) split stalled it. It powered the early web (CGI scripts, IMDb, Booking.com) and survives in system administration, bioinformatics and legacy scripts.",
"bash": "Brian Fox wrote Bash in 1989 for the GNU Project as a free replacement for the Bourne shell, adding command history, line editing and other conveniences. It is the glue of Unix: you combine small programs with pipes, redirections and variables, and it is available on practically every Linux server and Mac. As a programming language it is fragile: quoting rules are treacherous, it only does integer arithmetic and error handling is weak. It runs build, deploy and CI scripts and server administration everywhere, and AI coding agents use it constantly.",
"haskell": "A committee of researchers designed Haskell in 1990 to unify the many lazy, purely functional research languages of the 1980s into one open standard. Functions have no side effects, evaluation is lazy, and a strong type system with type classes catches whole classes of bugs before the program runs. The learning curve is steep (monads), performance is hard to predict because of laziness, and the job market is small. It is used in research, compilers, finance and tools such as Pandoc, and its ideas shaped Rust, Scala, Swift, Python and Excel's LAMBDA.",
"python": "Guido van Rossum created Python at CWI in Amsterdam, starting in 1989 and releasing it in 1991, as a readable scripting language building on his work on ABC. Indentation-based syntax, a large standard library and an enormous package ecosystem (NumPy, pandas, PyTorch) make it easy to learn and very productive. Pure Python is slow, so speed depends on C extensions, and dynamic typing lets errors slip through to runtime. It dominates data science, machine learning and AI, and is widely used for scripting, automation, web back ends and teaching.",
"r": "Ross Ihaka and Robert Gentleman created R at the University of Auckland in 1993 as a free implementation of the S language, with Scheme's ideas inside. It is built for statistics: vectors, data frames, formulas and missing values are native, and CRAN offers over 20,000 packages, from mixed models and survey methods to ggplot2. It can be slow and memory-hungry, has quirks from three object systems and is less suited to production software than Python. Its pipe, first %>% in the magrittr package (2014) that dplyr made famous and since R 4.1 the native |>, was borrowed from F#, while dplyr's verbs mirror SQL. It is used in academic statistics, biostatistics and pharma, survey research, epidemiology and data journalism.",
"java": "James Gosling and his team at Sun Microsystems created Java in 1995, first for consumer devices and then for the web. Code compiles to bytecode that runs on the Java Virtual Machine on any platform, with garbage collection and a large standard library, making it safer than C++ and highly portable. It is verbose, requires ceremony such as a class for everything, and the JVM uses a lot of memory. It runs enterprise back ends, banks, Android apps, big-data systems (Hadoop, Kafka) and Minecraft.",
"js": "Brendan Eich wrote JavaScript in ten days at Netscape in 1995 to make web pages interactive, borrowing Java's look, Scheme's functions and Self's prototypes. It runs in every browser with no installation, and with Node.js also on servers, giving it the largest developer community in the world. Its rushed design left quirks such as loose type coercion ('1' + 1 is '11') and a confusing 'this', many fixed since 2015. It runs every interactive website and much server code, increasingly written as TypeScript.",
"ruby": "Yukihiro 'Matz' Matsumoto created Ruby in Japan in 1995 as an object-oriented scripting language that puts programmer happiness first. Everything is an object, blocks make code read naturally, and Ruby on Rails (2004) made building web apps dramatically faster. It is slower than many rivals, its flexibility can make large codebases hard to follow, and its popularity has declined since the 2010s. It powers GitHub, Shopify, Basecamp, Homebrew and many startup web apps.",
"php": "Rasmus Lerdorf created PHP (originally Personal Home Page tools) at the University of Toronto in 1994 and 1995 to manage his website. It embeds directly in HTML and is trivial to deploy on cheap web hosting, which made it the default language of the early web. Its early design was inconsistent and insecure (function naming, type juggling), though PHP 7 and 8 fixed much of this. It powers WordPress, Wikipedia's MediaWiki, the original Facebook and a large share of all websites.",
"ocaml": "Xavier Leroy and colleagues at INRIA in France created OCaml in 1996, adding objects and a fast native compiler to the ML family. It combines strong static typing and type inference with practical performance, which makes it excellent for compilers, analysers and financial systems. Its ecosystem and community are small, multicore support arrived only in 2022, and tooling lagged behind mainstream languages. It is used at Jane Street, in Meta's code-analysis tools and in proof assistants such as Rocq, and it compiled Rust's first versions.",
"csharp": "Anders Hejlsberg designed C# at Microsoft in 2000 as the main language of the new .NET platform, Microsoft's answer to Java. It is a typed, modern language that adds features quickly (generics, LINQ queries, async/await) and has excellent tooling in Visual Studio. It was long tied to Windows, although .NET has been open source and cross-platform since 2016, and its ecosystem is more corporate than community-driven. It is used for enterprise back ends, Windows apps, Unity games and web services on Azure.",
"scala": "Martin Odersky created Scala at EPFL in Lausanne in 2004 to merge object-oriented and functional programming on the Java Virtual Machine. It is concise and expressive, with a powerful type system and full access to Java libraries, which is why Apache Spark and Kafka were written in it. It is complex, compiles slowly, offers many ways to write the same thing, and the move from Scala 2 to 3 split the community. It is used in big-data engineering, financial back ends and some web services, though data teams have largely moved to PySpark and SQL.",
"fsharp": "Don Syme created F# at Microsoft Research Cambridge in 2005 to bring OCaml-style functional programming to .NET. It offers type inference, immutable data and concise pipelines while using any .NET library, which suits finance and data processing. Its community is small, Microsoft invests far more in C#, and F# developers are hard to hire. It is used in finance, analytics and some back-end systems, and its pipe operator |>, which passes a value into the next function, spread to R (via magrittr and dplyr), Elixir, OCaml and others.",
"clojure": "Rich Hickey created Clojure in 2007 as a modern Lisp for the Java Virtual Machine, built around immutable data and safe concurrency. It is concise, interactive (development happens at a live REPL) and can use any Java library. The parentheses, slow JVM start-up and a small hiring pool limit adoption, and the community has shrunk since the mid-2010s. It runs Nubank's banking platform, Datomic and Metabase, and keeps a loyal following.",
"dax": "Microsoft introduced DAX (Data Analysis Expressions) in 2009 with Power Pivot for Excel; it was built by the Analysis Services team and now drives Power BI. It looks like Excel formulas but evaluates measures within a filter context, so one formula answers the question for any slice of the data. Filter and row context are hard to master, debugging is limited, and it only works inside Microsoft's tabular engine. It is used to build measures, KPIs and calculated columns in Power BI, Excel and Analysis Services.",
"go": "Robert Griesemer, Rob Pike and Ken Thompson designed Go at Google in 2007 (released 2009), frustrated by slow C++ builds and complexity. It is deliberately small, compiles very fast to a single binary, has garbage collection and built-in concurrency with goroutines and channels. It is verbose in places (repetitive error handling), lacked generics until 2022 and is less expressive than Rust or Kotlin. It runs cloud infrastructure such as Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform and Prometheus, plus many back-end services.",
"dart": "Lars Bak and Kasper Lund created Dart at Google's Aarhus office in 2011, originally to replace JavaScript in browsers. Other browsers refused to adopt it, but Flutter (2017) gave it a new role: one codebase for iOS, Android, web and desktop apps, with instant hot reload during development. Outside Flutter it has almost no use, and its future depends on Google's commitment. It is used for Flutter apps such as Google Pay and BMW's app, and AngularDart powered Google's Ads and Merchant Center web front-ends.",
"kotlin": "JetBrains, with Andrey Breslav as lead designer, created Kotlin in 2011 as a pragmatic, more concise replacement for Java on the JVM. It is fully interoperable with Java, null-safe by design and far less verbose, and Google made it Android's preferred language in 2019. It compiles more slowly than Java, and outside Android and JVM back ends its multiplatform story is still maturing. It is used for most new Android apps, server back ends and Gradle build scripts.",
"julia": "Jeff Bezanson, Stefan Karpinski, Viral Shah and Alan Edelman created Julia at MIT in 2012 to solve the 'two-language problem' of prototyping in a slow language and rewriting in a fast one. It is as easy as Python or R yet compiles to fast code, with multiple dispatch and excellent numerical support. Slow start-up and compile latency, fewer mature packages and a small job market have held it back. It is used in climate modelling, pharmaceutical modelling, optimisation and scientific research.",
"ts": "Anders Hejlsberg led the design of TypeScript at Microsoft in 2012 to make large JavaScript codebases manageable. It adds optional static types to JavaScript and compiles to plain JavaScript, catching errors before code runs and giving editors excellent autocomplete. Its type system is complex, it needs a build step, and types disappear at runtime, so they guarantee nothing about incoming data. It became GitHub's most used language in 2025 and is used for web front-ends (Angular, React), Node.js servers and tools such as VS Code and Claude Code.",
"elixir": "José Valim created Elixir in Brazil in 2012 to bring a friendly, Ruby-like syntax and modern tooling to Erlang's battle-tested virtual machine. It inherits Erlang's lightweight processes and fault tolerance and adds macros, an excellent package manager and the Phoenix web framework. It is not suited to heavy number crunching, its community is small, and dynamic typing (a gradual type system is arriving) limits tooling. It is used for real-time systems, chat and messaging (Discord) and web back ends.",
"m": "Microsoft created the M language in 2013 for Power Query, first as an Excel add-in and now in Power BI and Fabric. It is a functional, lazily evaluated language in which each step transforms a table, and most users generate it by clicking in the Power Query editor without ever seeing the code. Writing it by hand is awkward, it is case-sensitive unlike DAX, and performance tuning (query folding) is opaque. It is used to import, clean and reshape data before modelling in Excel and Power BI.",
"swift": "Chris Lattner started Swift at Apple in 2010 (released 2014) to replace Objective-C with a modern, safe and fast language. It has type inference, optionals instead of null pointers, value types and concise syntax, and compiles through LLVM to fast code. It is tied mostly to Apple's platforms, compile times can be slow, and the language changed rapidly in its early years. It is used for iPhone, iPad, Mac, Watch and Vision Pro apps, plus some server-side code.",
"rust": "Graydon Hoare started Rust as a personal project in 2006; Mozilla sponsored it from 2009 and version 1.0 arrived in 2015. Its ownership and borrowing rules, checked at compile time, give memory safety without a garbage collector, so it is as fast as C++ while avoiding most of its crashes and security holes. The learning curve is steep, compile times are long and the borrow checker rejects many programs at first. It is used for systems software, Firefox, cloud infrastructure, the Linux kernel, command-line tools and fast Python libraries (Polars, uv, Ruff).",
"simula": "Ole-Johan Dahl and Kristen Nygaard created Simula at the Norwegian Computing Center in the 1960s (Simula 67) to write simulations, as an extension of ALGOL 60. It invented classes, objects, inheritance and virtual methods, the foundation of object-oriented programming. It was slow on the hardware of its day and never gained wide commercial use. It was used for simulation and teaching, mainly in Scandinavia, and Dahl and Nygaard won the 2001 Turing Award because C++, Smalltalk and Java grew from it.",
"bcpl": "Martin Richards designed BCPL at the University of Cambridge in 1967 as a simplified version of CPL that was easy to compile and port. It had a single data type (the machine word) and braces for blocks, which kept it small and portable, at the cost of any type safety. It was used for compilers, the Xerox Alto's software and early games such as MUD. Ken Thompson's B stripped it down further, and B became C.",
"clu": "Barbara Liskov and her students at MIT created CLU in 1974 to explore data abstraction: defining new types by what they do rather than how they are built. It introduced iterators with yield, multiple return values and exceptions in a clean form. It remained a research language without industrial adoption. Its ideas shaped Ada, C++, Python, Ruby, Lua and Swift, and Liskov received the 2008 Turing Award partly for it.",
"awk": "Alfred Aho, Peter Weinberger and Brian Kernighan created AWK at Bell Labs in 1977; the name is their initials. It processes text line by line: for every line matching a pattern, it runs an action, with built-in fields and associative arrays. It is perfect for one-liners but awkward for anything large. It is still on every Unix-like system for reports and log processing, and it inspired Perl.",
"sh": "Stephen Bourne wrote the Bourne shell at Bell Labs in 1979 for Version 7 Unix, replacing Ken Thompson's simpler shell with a real scripting language. It added variables, functions and control structures with ALGOL 68-style keywords (if ... fi, case ... esac), turning shell scripts into programs. It is minimal and full of quoting pitfalls, but as the POSIX sh standard it runs everywhere. It is used for portable scripts and system start-up, and Bash, Zsh and Dash descend from it.",
"ada": "Jean Ichbiah's team at CII Honeywell Bull in France designed Ada (1980) after the US Department of Defense ran a competition to replace the hundreds of languages used in its systems. Named after Ada Lovelace, it emphasises strong typing, modules, generics and concurrency, catching errors at compile time in software that must not fail. It is verbose, compilers were long expensive, and few developers know it outside its niche. It is used in avionics (Boeing 777), rail signalling, air-traffic control, defence and space, and its syntax shaped Oracle's PL/SQL.",
"miranda": "David Turner created Miranda at the University of Kent in 1985 as a lazy, purely functional language with a clean, mathematical syntax. It made lazy evaluation and polymorphic types practical and pleasant to use. It was a commercial product with a licence fee, which pushed researchers to design an open alternative, Haskell. It was used in teaching and research in the late 1980s and is now historical, though Haskell's syntax owes it a great deal.",
"abc": "Leo Geurts, Lambert Meertens and Steven Pemberton designed ABC at CWI in Amsterdam in the 1980s as a teaching language to replace BASIC. It had indentation-based blocks, a few powerful built-in types (lists, dictionaries) and an integrated environment. It was too closed to catch on, because it could not easily work with files or the operating system. It is remembered as Python's parent: Guido van Rossum worked on ABC before creating Python.",
"self": "David Ungar and Randall Smith created Self at Xerox PARC in 1987, continuing it at Stanford and Sun, as a simpler Smalltalk without classes: new objects are made by copying prototypes. Its research team invented adaptive just-in-time compilation techniques that made dynamic languages fast. It never left the research lab. Its prototypes live on in JavaScript, and its compiler techniques went into Java's HotSpot JVM and Chrome's V8.",
"oberon": "Niklaus Wirth and Jürg Gutknecht created Oberon at ETH Zurich in 1987 as both a language and a complete operating system, written by the two of them. It took Modula-2 and removed everything nonessential, to show that a whole system can be simple and understandable. It stayed academic, with few users outside ETH. It was used for teaching and research, and it influenced Go through Robert Griesemer, who wrote his doctorate on an Oberon extension.",
"basic": "John Kemeny and Thomas Kurtz created BASIC (Beginner's All-purpose Symbolic Instruction Code) at Dartmouth College in 1964 so that non-science students could use time-sharing computers. It was simple to learn, with line numbers and plain commands, and it was built into almost every home computer of the 1970s and 1980s. Classic BASIC encouraged tangled GOTO code, which Dijkstra famously criticised, and had no structure for large programs. It taught a generation to program, was Microsoft's first product and evolved into Visual Basic.",
"logo": "Wally Feurzeig, Seymour Papert and Cynthia Solomon created Logo at BBN and MIT in 1967 to teach children mathematical thinking. It is essentially Lisp without parentheses, best known for turtle graphics, where children steer a turtle to draw shapes. It was seen as a children's language and had little use outside education. It shaped educational computing worldwide and led directly to Scratch.",
"vb": "Microsoft released Visual Basic in 1991, building on Alan Cooper's visual form designer: you drew windows with buttons and wrote BASIC code behind them. It made Windows programming accessible to millions and popularised rapid application development. It tended to produce hard-to-maintain code, VB.NET (2002) broke compatibility with VB6, and Microsoft stopped evolving the language in 2020. It lives on mainly as VBA, the macro language of Excel, Access and Office.",
"delphi": "Borland released Delphi in 1995, with Anders Hejlsberg as chief architect, as an Object Pascal environment for building Windows applications visually. It combined Visual Basic's drag-and-drop ease with fast, compiled native programs and a rich component library. Borland's decline, high licence costs and the rise of .NET and the web reduced it to a niche. It is used for long-lived business applications and tools such as FL Studio and Total Commander, and Skype's first Windows client was written in it.",
"lua": "Roberto Ierusalimschy, Luiz Henrique de Figueiredo and Waldemar Celes created Lua at PUC-Rio in Brazil in 1993, first for Petrobras engineering tools. It is tiny, fast and designed to be embedded inside other programs, with a single flexible data structure, the table. Its standard library is minimal, arrays start at 1 and it lacks the large ecosystem of Python. It is used for game scripting (Roblox, World of Warcraft add-ons), Neovim configuration and embedded devices.",
"scratch": "Mitchel Resnick's Lifelong Kindergarten group at the MIT Media Lab released Scratch in 2007 so children could make games and stories by snapping visual blocks together. It removes syntax errors entirely and encourages sharing and remixing in an online community of millions. It is limited for larger programs and is meant as a first step rather than a professional tool. It is used in schools and coding clubs worldwide as many children's first programming language.",
"zig": "Andrew Kelley created Zig in 2016 as a simpler, safer replacement for C. It has no hidden control flow or hidden memory allocations, powerful compile-time code execution, and it can compile and cross-compile C code with ease. It has not reached version 1.0, breaking changes are common and the ecosystem is young. It is used for performance-critical tools such as the Bun JavaScript runtime, the TigerBeetle database and the Ghostty terminal.",
"mojo": "Chris Lattner's company Modular introduced Mojo in 2023 as a superset of Python that runs at C-like speed on CPUs, GPUs and AI accelerators. It keeps Python's syntax while adding types, ownership and low-level control, aiming to remove the need to drop into C++ or CUDA. It is young, only partly open source and has little existing code for AI models to learn from. It is used mainly to write high-performance AI kernels for Modular's MAX platform.",
"pli": "IBM designed PL/I (Programming Language One) from 1964, with its users' group SHARE, to replace both Fortran and COBOL with one language for the new System/360. It combined scientific computing, business records, exception handling and multitasking. It was enormous and hard to compile, so it never displaced either parent. It was used for Multics and IBM mainframe applications, some of which still run, and its syntax shaped SAS.",
"spss": "Norman Nie, Dale Bent and Hadlai Hull created SPSS (Statistical Package for the Social Sciences) at Stanford in 1968 so social scientists could analyse surveys without programming in Fortran. Its syntax is a sequence of English-like commands with DO IF, LOOP and a macro facility, which makes analyses reproducible, and its menus make it accessible to non-programmers. It is expensive, closed (owned by IBM since 2009) and much less flexible than R or Python. It is used in social science, psychology, market research and survey analysis.",
"sas": "Anthony Barr, James Goodnight, John Sall and Jane Helwig developed SAS at North Carolina State University from 1966 to analyse agricultural research data; SAS Institute was founded in 1976. Its DATA steps and PROCs process very large datasets reliably, and its validated procedures became the standard for regulatory work. Licences are expensive, the language is idiosyncratic, and talent is shifting to R and Python. It is used in pharmaceutical clinical trials, banking, insurance and government statistics.",
"stata": "William Gould created Stata in 1985 at the Computing Resource Center (now StataCorp) to give researchers fast, reliable econometric tools. Its command syntax is concise and consistent, the documentation is outstanding, and it handles panel data, survey data and regression extremely well, with Mata as a full matrix language. It is commercial, traditionally works with one dataset in memory at a time and is less flexible than R for graphics and custom methods. It is used in applied economics, public health, political science and policy research.",
"abap": "SAP created ABAP in 1983 as a report language for its R/2 mainframe software, and it became the language of all SAP business applications. It is tightly integrated with SAP's database and business objects, so finance, logistics and HR processes can be customised inside the system. It is verbose and COBOL-like, useful only inside SAP, and its developers are a specialised pool. It is used to customise and extend SAP ERP and S/4HANA at large companies worldwide.",
"modula3": "A team at Digital Equipment Corporation's Systems Research Center and Olivetti Research designed Modula-3 in 1988 as a safer, object-oriented successor to Wirth's Modula-2. It combined modules, objects, exceptions, garbage collection and threads in a clean, simple language. It had no corporate champion after DEC's decline and never gained a user base. It is remembered for its influence on Python's modules, exceptions and classes, and on parts of Java's design.",
"hack": "Meta (then Facebook) created Hack in 2014, with Julien Verlaguet among its lead designers, to add static types to its enormous PHP codebase without stopping development. It adds gradual typing, generics and async functions to PHP and runs on Meta's HHVM virtual machine, with a type checker written in OCaml. Outside Meta it has little adoption, and it has diverged from mainstream PHP. It runs Facebook's and Messenger's web back ends, and Slack migrated its back end to Hack.",
"powershell": "Jeffrey Snover led the creation of PowerShell at Microsoft in 2006 to give Windows a powerful command-line automation tool. Unlike Unix shells, its commands pass structured .NET objects through pipelines rather than plain text, with consistent verb-noun names such as Get-Process. It is verbose and slower to start than Bash, and on Linux it remains a minority choice. It is used to administer Windows servers, Active Directory, Azure and Microsoft 365.",
"coffee": "Jeremy Ashkenas created CoffeeScript in 2009 as a cleaner syntax that compiles to JavaScript, borrowing from Ruby and Python. It offered arrow functions, classes, indentation-based blocks and less punctuation at a time when JavaScript lacked them. Once JavaScript adopted most of these features in 2015, the extra compile step lost its point, and debugging the compiled output was always awkward. It was used by GitHub's Atom editor, Dropbox and many Rails projects in the early 2010s; most have since moved to TypeScript.",
"mathematica": "Stephen Wolfram released Mathematica in 1988 through Wolfram Research; its language is now called the Wolfram Language. Everything is a symbolic expression, so it handles algebra, calculus and exact mathematics as well as numerics, with thousands of built-in functions and curated data. It is proprietary and expensive, and its unusual syntax rarely leaves its notebooks. It is used in physics, mathematics and engineering research and in education, it powers Wolfram|Alpha, and its notebook interface inspired Jupyter.",
"tex": "Donald Knuth created TeX at Stanford in 1978 because he was unhappy with the typesetting of his book series The Art of Computer Programming. It produces superb typography, especially for mathematics, and is a full, if odd, macro programming language; Leslie Lamport's LaTeX (1984) made it practical for authors. Error messages are cryptic, macros are hard to debug and the layout is not visual. It is the standard for mathematics, physics and computer-science papers, theses and books.",
"modula2": "Niklaus Wirth designed Modula-2 at ETH Zurich in 1978 as the successor to Pascal, to write the operating system of the Lilith workstation. Its key addition was modules with separate compilation and clear interfaces, which made Pascal-style programming suitable for large systems. It was overshadowed by C, C++ and Turbo Pascal's popular extensions. It was used in teaching and some embedded and industrial systems, and it led to Oberon and Modula-3.",
"eiffel": "Bertrand Meyer created Eiffel in 1986 at Interactive Software Engineering in California to make object-oriented software reliable. Its central idea is design by contract: routines state preconditions, postconditions and invariants that are checked as the program runs. It has a small community, commercial tooling and little industry adoption. It is used in teaching (Meyer later taught at ETH Zurich) and in some finance and defence systems, and its ideas influenced C#, Java and Ruby.",
"icon": "Ralph Griswold created Icon at the University of Arizona in 1977 as the successor to SNOBOL, for text and string processing. Its expressions can generate a series of results and backtrack automatically when one fails, a style called goal-directed evaluation. It remained a niche research language. Its generators directly inspired Python's yield.",
"snobol": "David Farber, Ralph Griswold and Ivan Polonsky created SNOBOL at Bell Labs in 1962 for manipulating strings of text. Pattern matching was built into the language, far more powerful than the regular expressions of the time, and SNOBOL4 added associative tables. It was slow, and its control flow based on success and failure jumps was unusual. It was used in humanities computing and text processing, and its ideas passed to AWK, Icon and Perl.",
"tsql": "Sybase created Transact-SQL (T-SQL) in 1987 for its SQL Server database, and Microsoft took it over when it licensed and later rewrote SQL Server. It adds variables, loops, error handling and stored procedures to SQL, so business logic can run inside the database next to the data. It is tied to Microsoft's (and Sybase's) databases, and procedural code inside a database is hard to test and version. It is used in Microsoft SQL Server, Azure SQL and Fabric warehouses for stored procedures, ETL jobs and reporting.",
"plsql": "Oracle introduced PL/SQL in 1988 with Oracle 6 as a procedural extension to SQL, with syntax modelled on Ada. It adds blocks, variables, loops, exceptions and packages, so whole applications can run inside the Oracle database close to the data. It only runs on Oracle, and logic locked inside the database is hard to migrate and test. It is used in banks, telecoms and government systems built on Oracle, and in Oracle's own business applications.",
"mdx": "Microsoft created MDX (Multidimensional Expressions) in 1997, with Mosha Pasumansky as its lead architect, to query OLAP cubes in what became SQL Server Analysis Services. It extends SQL-like SELECT syntax to axes, hierarchies and members, so analysts can slice multidimensional data. It is notoriously hard to learn and has been displaced by tabular models and DAX. It still runs older Analysis Services cubes and the Excel pivot tables connected to them.",
"cypher": "Andrés Taylor and the team at Neo4j, a company founded in Malmö, Sweden, created Cypher in 2011 to query graph databases. Its patterns look like ASCII-art diagrams, (person)-[:KNOWS]->(friend), which makes relationships easy to read and query, and it became the basis of the ISO GQL standard in 2024. It only applies to graph data, and very large graph traversals can be slow. It is used for fraud detection, recommendation engines, knowledge graphs and network analysis.",
"kql": "Microsoft's Israel R&D Center created KQL (Kusto Query Language) for Kusto, an internal engine for analysing logs and telemetry, later released as Azure Data Explorer. Queries are pipelines of steps (Table | where ... | summarize ...), which makes exploring huge volumes of log data fast and readable. It works only in Microsoft's data services. It is used in Azure Monitor, Microsoft Sentinel and Defender security hunting, and Fabric Real-Time Intelligence.",
"lookml": "Lloyd Tabb created LookML at Looker in 2012 as a modelling language that describes data once (dimensions, measures, joins) and generates the SQL for every question users ask. It brought the semantic layer to BI: consistent metric definitions kept under version control. It only works inside Looker, and large models become hard to maintain. It is used by companies on Looker (part of Google Cloud since 2020) to define governed metrics and dashboards.",
}
